1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,050
November 14th, 1957.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:04,770
A cold, gray Thursday,

3
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:08,770
in a farming town so small that most
Americans had never heard its name.

4
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:10,510
Up a muddy country road,

5
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:15,050
behind a stone house on a hill,
sit dozens of long, black cars.

6
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:15,850
Cadillacs.

7
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:16,650
Lincolns.

8
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:17,810
Imperials.

9
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:19,870
Their license plates read New York...

10
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:20,830
New Jersey...

11
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:21,890
Ohio...

12
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:22,750
Illinois...

13
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:23,550
Texas...

14
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:24,530
California...

15
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:25,510
Florida.

16
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:29,250
Inside, around a barbecue
pit and a mountain of steaks,

17
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:32,530
stand some of the most powerful
criminals in the United States.

18
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:34,590
And then, someone looks
down the driveway...

19
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:37,270
and sees a car that should not be there.

20
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:41,430
Within minutes, men in silk suits and
handmade shoes are running across cow

21
00:00:41,480 --> 00:00:42,350
pastures.

22
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:43,810
Crashing through the brush.

23
00:00:43,860 --> 00:00:45,250
Disappearing into the woods.

24
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:50,130
Bosses who commanded small armies,
scrambling through the mud like schoolboys.

25
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:54,270
And waiting for them at the bottom of
the hill is one stubborn state trooper,

26
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:56,950
a man the FBI had ignored for years.

27
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:58,950
The meeting never even really began.

28
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,610
But by the end of that day,

29
00:01:00,660 --> 00:01:04,270
the most powerful lawman in America
would be forced to face something he had

30
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:06,210
brushed aside for decades.

31
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:07,630
The Mafia was real.

32
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:08,450
And it was national.

33
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:10,870
And the strangest part of this story?

34
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:12,870
Almost nobody went to prison for it.

35
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:14,730
This is Black Dossier.

36
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:15,230
And this...

37
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:16,420
is Apalachin.

38
00:01:19,241 --> 00:01:21,783
Chapter One. The Man Who
Said It Didn'T Exist.

39
00:01:21,833 --> 00:01:25,643
To understand why one afternoon in
the countryside mattered so much,

40
00:01:25,693 --> 00:01:29,323
you first have to understand the man
who ran American law enforcement.

41
00:01:29,373 --> 00:01:31,323
By 1957, J.

42
00:01:31,373 --> 00:01:34,683
Edgar Hoover had led the Bureau
for more than thirty years.

43
00:01:34,733 --> 00:01:37,183
He had built its legend in the 1930s,

44
00:01:37,233 --> 00:01:40,543
chasing bank robbers and
kidnappers across the Midwest.

45
00:01:40,593 --> 00:01:40,996
Dillinger.

46
00:01:41,046 --> 00:01:42,823
Baby Face Nelson.

47
00:01:42,873 --> 00:01:44,083
Pretty Boy Floyd.

48
00:01:44,133 --> 00:01:47,003
His agents were heroes in
newsreels and comic books.

49
00:01:47,053 --> 00:01:47,983
The G-Men.

50
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:51,363
But on one subject, Hoover
was strangely quiet.

51
00:01:51,413 --> 00:01:54,263
When people spoke of a
national crime organization,

52
00:01:54,313 --> 00:01:58,803
a secret brotherhood with families in
every major city, Hoover pushed back.

53
00:01:58,853 --> 00:02:00,443
Gangs existed, of course.

54
00:02:00,493 --> 00:02:01,883
Racketeers existed.

55
00:02:01,933 --> 00:02:05,723
But in his view, they were local
problems, for local police.

56
00:02:05,773 --> 00:02:07,703
The FBI had bigger enemies.

57
00:02:07,753 --> 00:02:08,523
Communists.

58
00:02:08,573 --> 00:02:09,443
Spies.

59
00:02:09,493 --> 00:02:10,623
Subversives.

60
00:02:10,673 --> 00:02:11,823
The Cold War.

61
00:02:11,873 --> 00:02:13,203
Not everyone agreed.

62
00:02:13,253 --> 00:02:15,323
At the Federal Bureau of Narcotics,

63
00:02:15,373 --> 00:02:18,873
agents had been warning for years
about a network of Sicilian and

64
00:02:18,923 --> 00:02:23,863
Italian-American criminals moving heroin,
money and favors across state lines.

65
00:02:23,913 --> 00:02:26,363
And in 1950 and 1951,

66
00:02:26,413 --> 00:02:30,523
a Senate committee led by Estes Kefauver
took the question to television.

67
00:02:30,573 --> 00:02:33,463
Millions of Americans
watched gangsters testify.

68
00:02:33,513 --> 00:02:36,983
One of them, Frank Costello,
refused to let his face be filmed.

69
00:02:37,033 --> 00:02:39,703
So the cameras showed only his hands.

70
00:02:39,753 --> 00:02:42,563
Nervous, restless hands,
drumming the table,

71
00:02:42,613 --> 00:02:45,903
folding paper, while he dodged
question after question.

72
00:02:45,953 --> 00:02:50,083
The Kefauver Committee concluded that
a nationwide crime syndicate did exist.

73
00:02:50,133 --> 00:02:52,963
And still, the FBI kept its distance.

74
00:02:53,013 --> 00:02:55,743
Historians have argued
for decades about why.

75
00:02:55,793 --> 00:02:58,063
Some point to Hoover's
obsession with statistics,

76
00:02:58,113 --> 00:03:01,983
and his fear of long, messy
cases that might never be won.

77
00:03:02,033 --> 00:03:06,103
Others point to his worry that agents
working near the mob could be corrupted.

78
00:03:06,153 --> 00:03:09,463
Whatever the reason,
by the autumn of 1957,

79
00:03:09,513 --> 00:03:13,123
the most famous law enforcement agency
in the world knew remarkably little

80
00:03:13,173 --> 00:03:16,323
about the men who would soon gather
on a hill in upstate New York.

81
00:03:16,373 --> 00:03:18,653
But one state trooper knew a great deal.

82
00:03:20,457 --> 00:03:22,623
Chapter Two. The Bottler On Mcfall Road.

83
00:03:22,673 --> 00:03:27,523
Apalachin sits in New York's Southern
Tier, close to the Pennsylvania border.

84
00:03:27,573 --> 00:03:29,623
Locals pronounce it ap-uh-LAY-kin.

85
00:03:29,673 --> 00:03:34,043
In the 1950s it was rolling hills,
dairy farms, and quiet roads.

86
00:03:34,093 --> 00:03:38,443
And on one of those roads, McFall
Road, lived a man named Joseph Barbara.

87
00:03:38,493 --> 00:03:42,163
Barbara was born in 1905
in Castellammare del Golfo,

88
00:03:42,213 --> 00:03:44,263
a coastal town in western Sicily.

89
00:03:44,313 --> 00:03:45,623
That town matters.

90
00:03:45,673 --> 00:03:48,823
It produced an entire
generation of American mafiosi.

91
00:03:48,873 --> 00:03:52,483
The men who would later lead powerful
families in Brooklyn and Buffalo traced

92
00:03:52,533 --> 00:03:54,113
their roots to the same harbor.

93
00:03:54,163 --> 00:03:56,383
Barbara came to America young.

94
00:03:56,433 --> 00:03:58,583
During Prohibition he
worked in bootlegging,

95
00:03:58,633 --> 00:04:00,843
in Pennsylvania and upstate New York.

96
00:04:00,893 --> 00:04:03,123
Over the years he was
arrested more than once,

97
00:04:03,173 --> 00:04:05,843
and his name came up in
serious investigations.

98
00:04:05,893 --> 00:04:07,443
But nothing stuck.

99
00:04:07,493 --> 00:04:12,003
By the 1950s, Joseph Barbara looked
like a respectable businessman.

100
00:04:12,053 --> 00:04:15,503
He held the local distributorship
for Canada Dry soft drinks.

101
00:04:15,553 --> 00:04:17,303
He owned a large estate on a hill,

102
00:04:17,353 --> 00:04:21,483
with a stone house, a big garage,
and plenty of space for guests.

103
00:04:21,533 --> 00:04:23,343
And he had a lot of guests.

104
00:04:23,393 --> 00:04:26,403
Barbara was known in the
underworld as a generous host.

105
00:04:26,453 --> 00:04:29,843
His estate was quiet, private,
far from New York City,

106
00:04:29,893 --> 00:04:32,443
and far from the eyes
of big-city detectives.

107
00:04:32,493 --> 00:04:35,663
For years, it had been used for
gatherings of men from across the region,

108
00:04:35,713 --> 00:04:37,083
and beyond.

109
00:04:37,133 --> 00:04:38,283
Somebody noticed.

110
00:04:38,333 --> 00:04:42,583
His name was Edgar Croswell, a sergeant
with the New York State Police.

111
00:04:42,633 --> 00:04:44,763
Croswell was not a celebrity detective.

112
00:04:44,813 --> 00:04:46,443
He was a working investigator,

113
00:04:46,493 --> 00:04:50,923
stationed nearby, who had become
quietly obsessed with Joseph Barbara.

114
00:04:50,973 --> 00:04:53,623
Barbara's official record
had been clean for years.

115
00:04:53,673 --> 00:04:57,383
But Croswell didn't buy the image
of the friendly soft drink man.

116
00:04:57,433 --> 00:04:58,523
As he later put it,

117
00:04:58,573 --> 00:05:01,823
he simply had a feeling that Barbara
was the big mobster in the area.

118
00:05:01,873 --> 00:05:03,423
So he watched.

119
00:05:03,473 --> 00:05:08,723
In 1956, Croswell pulled over a car
that had just left the Barbara estate.

120
00:05:08,773 --> 00:05:11,823
Behind the wheel was a man
with a long record in New York:

121
00:05:11,873 --> 00:05:16,123
Carmine Galante, a rising figure
in the Bonanno family of Brooklyn.

122
00:05:16,173 --> 00:05:19,523
Why would a Brooklyn gangster be leaving
a soft drink distributor's house in

123
00:05:19,573 --> 00:05:20,843
rural New York?

124
00:05:20,893 --> 00:05:23,183
Croswell kept asking that question.

125
00:05:23,233 --> 00:05:25,843
Nobody above him seemed very
interested in the answer.

126
00:05:25,893 --> 00:05:30,033
But the following year, the underworld
itself would give Croswell his chance.

127
00:05:30,897 --> 00:05:35,807
Chapter Three. 1957. The
Year The Underworld Shook.

128
00:05:35,857 --> 00:05:39,527
To understand why so many bosses
would travel hundreds of miles to a

129
00:05:39,577 --> 00:05:43,647
farmhouse, you need to know what
happened in New York in 1957.

130
00:05:43,697 --> 00:05:47,647
For years, the city's underworld had
been run by an uneasy balance of powerful

131
00:05:47,697 --> 00:05:48,767
men.

132
00:05:48,817 --> 00:05:51,247
One of the most powerful
was Frank Costello,

133
00:05:51,297 --> 00:05:54,547
the same man whose hands had
appeared on national television.

134
00:05:54,597 --> 00:05:57,207
Costello was called the Prime
Minister of the Underworld.

135
00:05:57,257 --> 00:06:01,167
He preferred deals to violence,
and politicians to gunmen.

136
00:06:01,217 --> 00:06:03,927
On the night of May 2nd, 1957,

137
00:06:03,977 --> 00:06:07,827
Costello walked into the lobby of his
apartment building on Central Park West.

138
00:06:07,877 --> 00:06:10,907
A man stepped out of the
shadows and fired a single shot.

139
00:06:10,957 --> 00:06:12,987
The bullet grazed Costello's head.

140
00:06:13,037 --> 00:06:14,307
He survived.

141
00:06:14,357 --> 00:06:18,367
In his pocket, police found a slip
of paper with handwritten figures.

142
00:06:18,417 --> 00:06:22,084
They would later be traced to the earnings
of a brand-new casino in Las Vegas,

143
00:06:22,134 --> 00:06:22,878
the Tropicana.

144
00:06:22,928 --> 00:06:26,847
A small piece of paper,
suggesting a very large secret.

145
00:06:26,897 --> 00:06:29,547
Costello refused to name his attacker.

146
00:06:29,597 --> 00:06:31,107
But the message was clear.

147
00:06:31,157 --> 00:06:32,387
Someone wanted him out.

148
00:06:32,437 --> 00:06:35,447
And soon, Costello
stepped back from power.

149
00:06:35,497 --> 00:06:39,307
The man most widely believed to
be behind it was Vito Genovese.

150
00:06:39,357 --> 00:06:41,667
Ambitious, patient, and ruthless,

151
00:06:41,717 --> 00:06:44,787
Genovese had waited years to take
control of what had once been Lucky

152
00:06:44,837 --> 00:06:46,367
Luciano's family.

153
00:06:46,417 --> 00:06:51,127
Then, on October 25th,
1957, came a second shock.

154
00:06:51,177 --> 00:06:54,907
Albert Anastasia, boss of one of
New York's most feared families,

155
00:06:54,957 --> 00:06:58,687
was killed in the barbershop of the
Park Sheraton Hotel in Manhattan.

156
00:06:58,737 --> 00:07:02,167
It was one of the most notorious
gangland killings of the century.

157
00:07:02,217 --> 00:07:04,007
The case was never solved.

158
00:07:04,057 --> 00:07:08,067
In just six months, the balance of
power in New York had been shattered.

159
00:07:08,117 --> 00:07:10,207
There were questions that needed answers.

160
00:07:10,257 --> 00:07:12,287
Who would control Anastasia's family?

161
00:07:12,337 --> 00:07:13,747
Who would sit at the table?

162
00:07:13,797 --> 00:07:17,207
What would happen to the lucrative
casino interests in Havana, Cuba?

163
00:07:17,257 --> 00:07:18,767
To the narcotics trade?

164
00:07:18,817 --> 00:07:21,227
To the trucking business in
the city's garment district?

165
00:07:21,277 --> 00:07:25,827
According to most accounts, Vito
Genovese wanted a national meeting.

166
00:07:25,877 --> 00:07:29,067
A gathering where bosses from across
the country could recognize the new

167
00:07:29,117 --> 00:07:31,907
order, settle disputes, and move on.

168
00:07:31,957 --> 00:07:34,107
The meeting needed a host who was trusted,

169
00:07:34,157 --> 00:07:36,987
discreet, and far away from the city.

170
00:07:37,037 --> 00:07:38,987
Joseph Barbara offered his home.

171
00:07:39,037 --> 00:07:43,287
The arrangements were reportedly overseen
by Russell Bufalino of Pennsylvania,

172
00:07:43,337 --> 00:07:44,947
Barbara's close associate.

173
00:07:44,997 --> 00:07:49,287
The date was set: Thursday,
November 14th, 1957.

174
00:07:49,337 --> 00:07:52,437
Bosses from New York, Pennsylvania, Ohio,

175
00:07:52,487 --> 00:07:56,647
Illinois, Florida, Texas, and
beyond began making plans.

176
00:07:56,697 --> 00:08:00,547
But the man they should have worried
about wasn't in New York or Chicago.

177
00:08:00,597 --> 00:08:03,977
He was standing in a motel
lobby, about ten miles away.

178
00:08:05,881 --> 00:08:07,047
Chapter Four. Room Reservations.

179
00:08:07,097 --> 00:08:10,757
On November 13th, the
day before the meeting,

180
00:08:10,807 --> 00:08:14,657
Sergeant Croswell was at the Parkway
Motel in the nearby town of Vestal,

181
00:08:14,707 --> 00:08:16,697
on an unrelated police matter.

182
00:08:16,747 --> 00:08:21,017
While he was there, he noticed a familiar
young man crossing the parking lot.

183
00:08:21,067 --> 00:08:22,837
It was Joseph Barbara Junior,

184
00:08:22,887 --> 00:08:26,077
the son of the man Croswell
had been watching for years.

185
00:08:26,127 --> 00:08:28,277
The younger Barbara was reserving rooms.

186
00:08:28,327 --> 00:08:29,157
Several of them.

187
00:08:29,207 --> 00:08:30,877
For out-of-town guests.

188
00:08:30,927 --> 00:08:33,193
For most police officers,
that would mean nothing.

189
00:08:33,243 --> 00:08:34,801
Families book rooms.

190
00:08:34,851 --> 00:08:36,917
Businesses host visitors.

191
00:08:36,967 --> 00:08:39,457
But Croswell knew who Joseph Barbara was.

192
00:08:39,507 --> 00:08:41,937
And he knew who had
visited that house before.

193
00:08:41,987 --> 00:08:43,557
Then came another detail.

194
00:08:43,607 --> 00:08:44,997
Around the same time,

195
00:08:45,047 --> 00:08:48,257
Croswell learned that a large meat order
had been placed with a local butcher

196
00:08:48,307 --> 00:08:49,757
for the Barbara home.

197
00:08:49,807 --> 00:08:52,677
Roughly two hundred pounds
of steaks and chops.

198
00:08:52,727 --> 00:08:54,477
Rooms for out-of-town guests.

199
00:08:54,527 --> 00:08:56,397
A feast big enough for an army.

200
00:08:56,447 --> 00:09:00,237
At the home of a man Croswell
believed was a regional crime boss.

201
00:09:00,287 --> 00:09:01,917
Croswell didn't have a crime.

202
00:09:01,967 --> 00:09:03,217
He didn't have a warrant.

203
00:09:03,267 --> 00:09:05,817
What he had was a hunch,
and a pair of eyes.

204
00:09:05,867 --> 00:09:08,707
So the next morning, he
decided to go and take a look.

205
00:09:09,475 --> 00:09:11,265
Chapter Five. The Roadblock.

206
00:09:11,315 --> 00:09:15,025
On November 14th, Croswell
set out with his partner,

207
00:09:15,075 --> 00:09:18,065
Trooper Vincent Vasisko,
and two agents from the U.S.

208
00:09:18,115 --> 00:09:19,265
Treasury Department.

209
00:09:19,315 --> 00:09:22,045
They drove up McFall
Road in an unmarked car.

210
00:09:22,095 --> 00:09:24,305
What they saw stopped them cold.

211
00:09:24,355 --> 00:09:27,965
Parked around Barbara's property
were dozens of expensive cars.

212
00:09:28,015 --> 00:09:30,305
Big, dark, freshly polished.

213
00:09:30,355 --> 00:09:32,525
License plates from all over the country.

214
00:09:32,575 --> 00:09:35,125
In a town where most
people drove pickup trucks,

215
00:09:35,175 --> 00:09:38,225
it looked like a luxury car showroom
had landed in a cow pasture.

216
00:09:38,275 --> 00:09:41,685
The officers began writing
down license numbers.

217
00:09:41,735 --> 00:09:43,885
At around twenty to one in the afternoon,

218
00:09:43,935 --> 00:09:47,095
someone inside the house noticed
the unmarked car in the driveway.

219
00:09:47,145 --> 00:09:51,205
According to later court records,
it was Barbara's wife who saw it.

220
00:09:51,255 --> 00:09:53,545
Word spread through the
property in seconds.

221
00:09:53,595 --> 00:09:55,245
Croswell knew what came next.

222
00:09:55,295 --> 00:09:56,665
He called for backup.

223
00:09:56,715 --> 00:09:58,605
More troopers raced to the area,

224
00:09:58,655 --> 00:10:01,845
and the police set up a roadblock
on the road below the estate,

225
00:10:01,895 --> 00:10:04,105
the only practical way out by car.

226
00:10:04,155 --> 00:10:07,345
Then, at about twenty past
one, the exodus began.

227
00:10:07,395 --> 00:10:11,085
Some of the guests jumped into
their cars and drove down the hill,

228
00:10:11,135 --> 00:10:12,845
straight into the roadblock.

229
00:10:12,895 --> 00:10:15,798
The first car to arrive carried
two men from the region,

230
00:10:15,848 --> 00:10:18,825
Emanuele Zicari and Dominick Alaimo.

231
00:10:18,875 --> 00:10:20,585
Others chose a different escape.

232
00:10:20,635 --> 00:10:21,485
They ran.

233
00:10:21,535 --> 00:10:22,325
Picture it.

234
00:10:22,375 --> 00:10:24,045
Men who controlled unions,

235
00:10:24,095 --> 00:10:26,185
casinos and entire city districts,

236
00:10:26,235 --> 00:10:29,305
sprinting across open fields
in tailored overcoats.

237
00:10:29,355 --> 00:10:31,665
Expensive shoes sinking into mud.

238
00:10:31,715 --> 00:10:33,165
Hats lost in the brush.

239
00:10:33,215 --> 00:10:35,285
Some headed for the woods along the creek,

240
00:10:35,335 --> 00:10:38,065
hoping to slip away and
catch a ride on a back road.

241
00:10:38,115 --> 00:10:39,285
Some of them made it.

242
00:10:39,335 --> 00:10:40,305
Many did not.

243
00:10:40,355 --> 00:10:41,305
One by one,

244
00:10:41,355 --> 00:10:44,785
the troopers stopped cars and gathered
the men who came stumbling out of the

245
00:10:44,835 --> 00:10:45,785
trees.

246
00:10:45,835 --> 00:10:49,645
Every one of them was taken to the state
police barracks for identification.

247
00:10:49,695 --> 00:10:53,145
By the end of the day, the police
had identified fifty-eight men.

248
00:10:53,195 --> 00:10:56,585
Estimates suggest that around a
hundred had come to the meeting.

249
00:10:56,635 --> 00:10:58,225
Dozens escaped on foot.

250
00:10:58,275 --> 00:11:00,885
Among the names later linked
to those who got away,

251
00:11:00,935 --> 00:11:04,125
writers have often mentioned
Sam Giancana of Chicago.

252
00:11:04,175 --> 00:11:07,005
But the men who were caught
were extraordinary enough.

253
00:11:07,055 --> 00:11:10,615
Vito Genovese, the man at the
center of New York's power struggle.

254
00:11:10,665 --> 00:11:14,685
Carlo Gambino, soon to become one of
the most powerful bosses in American

255
00:11:14,735 --> 00:11:15,745
history.

256
00:11:15,795 --> 00:11:19,205
Joseph Profaci and Joseph
Magliocco of Brooklyn.

257
00:11:19,255 --> 00:11:21,925
Santo Trafficante, with
his interests in Havana.

258
00:11:21,975 --> 00:11:25,085
Joseph Civello, representing Dallas.

259
00:11:25,135 --> 00:11:27,005
Russell Bufalino, the organizer.

260
00:11:27,055 --> 00:11:28,985
And their host, Joseph Barbara.

261
00:11:29,035 --> 00:11:30,645
In a single afternoon,

262
00:11:30,695 --> 00:11:34,125
a state trooper in a small town
had done what the FBI had not.

263
00:11:34,175 --> 00:11:37,725
He had put names, faces and
addresses to a national network.

264
00:11:37,775 --> 00:11:39,105
Now came the hard part.

265
00:11:39,155 --> 00:11:41,135
Explaining what they were all doing there.

266
00:11:43,059 --> 00:11:45,080
Chapter Six. Visiting A Sick Friend.

267
00:11:45,130 --> 00:11:49,040
At the barracks, the troopers asked
each man the same simple question.

268
00:11:49,090 --> 00:11:50,600
Why are you in Apalachin?

269
00:11:50,650 --> 00:11:52,380
And one by one, these men,

270
00:11:52,430 --> 00:11:54,820
from different cities, different families,

271
00:11:54,870 --> 00:11:58,280
different businesses, gave
remarkably similar answers.

272
00:11:58,330 --> 00:12:00,480
They had come to visit an old friend.

273
00:12:00,530 --> 00:12:02,500
Joseph Barbara had a heart condition.

274
00:12:02,550 --> 00:12:04,160
They had heard he was unwell.

275
00:12:04,210 --> 00:12:06,440
They stopped by to see how he was doing.

276
00:12:06,490 --> 00:12:09,420
It was true that Barbara had suffered
a heart attack the year before.

277
00:12:09,470 --> 00:12:12,200
But the idea that dozens of men had all,

278
00:12:12,250 --> 00:12:16,320
by coincidence, chosen the same Thursday
to check on his health was hard to

279
00:12:16,370 --> 00:12:17,600
take seriously.

280
00:12:17,650 --> 00:12:20,180
Some described themselves as businessmen.

281
00:12:20,230 --> 00:12:21,560
Olive oil importers.

282
00:12:21,610 --> 00:12:23,040
Dress manufacturers.

283
00:12:23,090 --> 00:12:24,300
Restaurant owners.

284
00:12:24,350 --> 00:12:26,180
Taxi company operators.

285
00:12:26,230 --> 00:12:28,460
Together, according to later reports,

286
00:12:28,510 --> 00:12:31,740
the men had criminal histories adding
up to more than two hundred and

287
00:12:31,790 --> 00:12:35,000
seventy-five arrests and
over one hundred convictions.

288
00:12:35,050 --> 00:12:37,300
And yet, the officers
faced a real problem.

289
00:12:37,350 --> 00:12:40,100
Attending a barbecue was not a crime.

290
00:12:40,150 --> 00:12:42,220
Visiting a friend was not a crime.

291
00:12:42,270 --> 00:12:45,080
Driving across state
lines was not a crime.

292
00:12:45,130 --> 00:12:49,480
Running from a party was embarrassing,
but it was not, by itself, a crime.

293
00:12:49,530 --> 00:12:50,880
Croswell had their names.

294
00:12:50,930 --> 00:12:52,120
He had their faces.

295
00:12:52,170 --> 00:12:54,580
He had their addresses
and their license plates.

296
00:12:54,630 --> 00:12:57,240
What he didn't have was
a charge that would hold.

297
00:12:57,290 --> 00:13:01,180
So, after they were identified and
questioned, the men were released.

298
00:13:01,230 --> 00:13:02,600
It looked like a defeat.

299
00:13:02,650 --> 00:13:03,510
It wasn't.

300
00:13:04,167 --> 00:13:06,657
Chapter Seven. The Fallout.

301
00:13:06,707 --> 00:13:09,637
The next morning, the
story was everywhere.

302
00:13:09,687 --> 00:13:13,037
Newspapers across the country
ran it on their front pages.

303
00:13:13,087 --> 00:13:14,977
Reporters raced to Apalachin.

304
00:13:15,027 --> 00:13:17,997
Photographers stood outside
the stone house on the hill.

305
00:13:18,047 --> 00:13:22,017
And Americans who had dismissed the
word "Mafia" as a movie cliché started

306
00:13:22,067 --> 00:13:23,517
asking a question:

307
00:13:23,567 --> 00:13:26,377
If all these men from all
these cities knew each other,

308
00:13:26,427 --> 00:13:28,977
and all came to the same
house on the same day...

309
00:13:29,027 --> 00:13:30,937
what exactly were they part of?

310
00:13:30,987 --> 00:13:33,937
The pressure landed squarely on the FBI.

311
00:13:33,987 --> 00:13:37,697
The Federal Bureau of Narcotics
had files on many of these men.

312
00:13:37,747 --> 00:13:41,117
The FBI, by many accounts,
had surprisingly little.

313
00:13:41,167 --> 00:13:43,897
It was an embarrassment
Hoover could not ignore.

314
00:13:43,947 --> 00:13:47,997
Within two weeks, on November 27th, 1957,

315
00:13:48,047 --> 00:13:51,737
the Bureau sent out a directive expanding
what became known as the Top Hoodlum

316
00:13:51,787 --> 00:13:52,937
Program.

317
00:13:52,987 --> 00:13:56,497
Field offices across the country were
told to identify the leading racketeers

318
00:13:56,547 --> 00:14:00,737
in their territory and to gather
intelligence on them, systematically.

319
00:14:00,787 --> 00:14:03,657
Historians still debate
how new this really was.

320
00:14:03,707 --> 00:14:08,497
The FBI had a smaller version of
the program on paper since 1953.

321
00:14:08,547 --> 00:14:12,737
But after Apalachin, the effort
changed in scale and seriousness.

322
00:14:12,787 --> 00:14:14,237
In the years that followed,

323
00:14:14,287 --> 00:14:17,937
agents would use informants and hidden
microphones to listen inside the mob's

324
00:14:17,987 --> 00:14:19,577
own meeting places.

325
00:14:19,627 --> 00:14:21,197
Washington reacted too.

326
00:14:21,247 --> 00:14:24,437
In the Senate, a young
lawyer named Robert F.

327
00:14:24,487 --> 00:14:27,977
Kennedy, chief counsel of the committee
investigating labor racketeering,

328
00:14:28,027 --> 00:14:31,097
saw Apalachin as proof of
what he had been arguing.

329
00:14:31,147 --> 00:14:33,197
A national conspiracy existed.

330
00:14:33,247 --> 00:14:36,417
He would carry that conviction with him
when he became Attorney General a few

331
00:14:36,467 --> 00:14:37,577
years later.

332
00:14:37,627 --> 00:14:39,217
As for the men themselves,

333
00:14:39,267 --> 00:14:41,957
the government tried to make
Apalachin a criminal case.

334
00:14:42,007 --> 00:14:46,797
In May 1959, a federal grand jury
indicted a group of the attendees.

335
00:14:46,847 --> 00:14:48,797
The charge was not attending the meeting.

336
00:14:48,847 --> 00:14:53,317
It was conspiracy to obstruct justice,
by lying about why they had been there.

337
00:14:53,367 --> 00:14:55,997
That December, twenty men were convicted.

338
00:14:56,047 --> 00:14:59,017
They received prison sentences
of three to five years,

339
00:14:59,067 --> 00:15:02,977
and thirteen of them were also
fined ten thousand dollars each.

340
00:15:03,027 --> 00:15:04,877
Prosecutors called it a landmark.

341
00:15:04,927 --> 00:15:06,357
It didn't last.

342
00:15:06,407 --> 00:15:08,757
On November 28th, 1960,

343
00:15:08,807 --> 00:15:12,097
the United States Court of Appeals
for the Second Circuit reversed every

344
00:15:12,147 --> 00:15:13,077
conviction.

345
00:15:13,127 --> 00:15:14,577
The reasoning was simple.

346
00:15:14,627 --> 00:15:17,497
The government admitted it could not
prove what the meeting had actually been

347
00:15:17,547 --> 00:15:18,457
about.

348
00:15:18,507 --> 00:15:20,857
And if you cannot prove
what the meeting was for,

349
00:15:20,907 --> 00:15:23,337
you cannot prove that
the men lied about it.

350
00:15:23,387 --> 00:15:27,097
Even some commentators who wanted the
mob behind bars agreed the charge had

351
00:15:27,147 --> 00:15:28,297
been a stretch.

352
00:15:28,347 --> 00:15:32,437
So, in the end, no one went to
prison for attending Apalachin.

353
00:15:32,487 --> 00:15:34,547
But that was never really the point.

354
00:15:36,339 --> 00:15:38,452
Chapter Eight. The
Trooper Who Woke A Nation.

355
00:15:38,502 --> 00:15:42,312
The meeting at Apalachin was supposed
to bring order to the underworld.

356
00:15:42,362 --> 00:15:44,352
Instead, it brought daylight.

357
00:15:44,402 --> 00:15:47,732
For the men involved, the
consequences came in other ways.

358
00:15:47,782 --> 00:15:52,192
Joseph Barbara, the host, found his
business life collapsing under scrutiny.

359
00:15:52,242 --> 00:15:55,152
He faced tax trouble,
lost his liquor license,

360
00:15:55,202 --> 00:15:57,452
and saw his legitimate business suffer.

361
00:15:57,502 --> 00:16:00,612
In June 1959, he died of a heart attack.

362
00:16:00,662 --> 00:16:03,332
His estate on McFall Road was later sold.

363
00:16:03,382 --> 00:16:07,632
Vito Genovese, the man many believe
called the meeting, did reach the top.

364
00:16:07,682 --> 00:16:09,092
But not for long.

365
00:16:09,142 --> 00:16:14,072
In 1959, he was convicted in a federal
narcotics case and sentenced to fifteen

366
00:16:14,122 --> 00:16:15,412
years in prison.

367
00:16:15,462 --> 00:16:17,812
He would die behind bars a decade later.

368
00:16:17,862 --> 00:16:21,192
Some in the underworld have long
whispered that the case against him was

369
00:16:21,242 --> 00:16:23,032
helped along by his own rivals.

370
00:16:23,082 --> 00:16:24,472
That has never been proven.

371
00:16:24,522 --> 00:16:26,272
And Sergeant Edgar Croswell?

372
00:16:26,322 --> 00:16:30,132
He became, for a time, one of the
most famous policemen in America.

373
00:16:30,182 --> 00:16:32,812
A small-town investigator
who trusted his instincts,

374
00:16:32,862 --> 00:16:36,832
followed a few rooms in a motel and
a meat order from a local butcher,

375
00:16:36,882 --> 00:16:40,552
and exposed something the most powerful
police agency in the country had

376
00:16:40,602 --> 00:16:42,352
downplayed for decades.

377
00:16:42,402 --> 00:16:46,232
The men at Apalachin had spent their
lives building an organization designed

378
00:16:46,282 --> 00:16:47,592
to stay invisible.

379
00:16:47,642 --> 00:16:48,792
No written records.

380
00:16:48,842 --> 00:16:50,152
No public faces.

381
00:16:50,202 --> 00:16:51,612
A code of silence.

382
00:16:51,662 --> 00:16:54,532
And it all came undone
because a few dozen men,

383
00:16:54,582 --> 00:16:57,192
in a moment of panic, ran into the woods.

384
00:16:57,242 --> 00:17:00,992
After that day, the word
"Mafia" was no longer a rumor.

385
00:17:01,042 --> 00:17:02,152
It was a headline.

386
00:17:02,202 --> 00:17:05,752
And the people hunting it now knew
exactly whose names to write down.

387
00:17:05,802 --> 00:17:07,992
But the biggest blow was still to come.

388
00:17:08,042 --> 00:17:09,212
Six years later,

389
00:17:09,262 --> 00:17:13,392
a low-level soldier from Vito Genovese's
own family would sit in front of a

390
00:17:13,442 --> 00:17:15,752
Senate committee, with
television cameras rolling,

391
00:17:15,802 --> 00:17:19,052
and do something no made
man had ever done in public.

392
00:17:19,102 --> 00:17:21,772
He would describe the
organization from the inside.

393
00:17:21,822 --> 00:17:22,712
Its ranks.

394
00:17:22,762 --> 00:17:23,312
Its rituals.

395
00:17:23,362 --> 00:17:24,792
Its name.

396
00:17:24,842 --> 00:17:26,692
His name was Joseph Valachi.

397
00:17:26,742 --> 00:17:27,192
And that...

398
00:17:27,242 --> 00:17:30,072
is a story for the next Black Dossier.

399
00:17:30,122 --> 00:17:33,332
If you want to see it, subscribe
and turn on notifications.

400
00:17:33,382 --> 00:17:35,952
And tell me in the comments: which family,

401
00:17:36,002 --> 00:17:38,732
which boss, or which
case should we open next?

402
00:17:38,782 --> 00:17:40,202
I'll see you in the next file.
